Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
And at that time, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Who is the greatest person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to Him. He asked the child to stand among them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't do that, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven.
"Everyone who welcomes a little child like this in my name, welcomes me."
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me causes one of them to sin? If he does that, he would be better off to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depth of the sea.
How great is the affliction that will come upon the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Such things must come. But how great is the affliction that will come upon those who cause them!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter life maimed or lame than to have two hands or two feet and be thrown into eternal fire. For the Son of Man will come in his glory, and then he will repay each person according to what they have done.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye, than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Look that you do not despise one of these little ones, for I say to you: Their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father who is in heaven.
Translate
What do you think? Let's suppose there was a man who owned a hundred sheep, and one of them went missing. Wouldn't he leave the ninety-nine sheep on the hills and go search for the one that went missing?
What I am going to tell you is true. If he finds that sheep, he rejoices over it more than he would rejoice over ninety-nine sheep that did not go astray.
The same applies to your father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. And keep it between the two of you. If he listens to you, you will have him back again.
But what if he does not listen to you? Then take along one or two others. The Bible says: "At the mouth of two or three witnesses every word shall be established" (Deuteronomy 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then report that to the church. And what if he refuses to even listen to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat any unbeliever or tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is truth. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And what you release on earth will be released in heaven.
And again, this is what I am telling you: if two of you agree on earth about anything they ask for, it will be done for them by my Father who is in heaven.
"Where two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them." The servant who had no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
And as the king began to carry this out, a man was brought to him who owed him millions of dollars.
The man could not pay. So his master issued a warrant. The man, his wife, his children, and all that he possessed were to be sold to pay what he owed.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me some time," he pleaded, "I will return everything."
So his master had compassion on him, and exempted him from the debt he owed, and left him to go.
But that waiter went out afterward and found another waiter to whom he owed a few dollars. He grabbed him and began to strangle him, saying, "Pay me what you owe me!"
And the other servant fell on his knees. He begged, "Give me some time, and I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused to do that. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. And the man would have stayed there until he was able to pay back what he owed.
And the other servants saw what happened. And that greatly disturbed them. So they went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called the first servant and said to him, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that debt because you pleaded with me.
Did it not occur to you that you should have compassion for the other servant, just as I have compassion on you?
So his master, being angry, delivered him to the guards. And he was to suffer punishment until he should pay all that he owed.
Thus will my heavenly Father treat each of you, unless you from your heart forgive your brother.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
And when Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the regions of Judea beyond the Jordan.
Large crowds followed him. And he healed them there.
And some Pharisees came to him, testing him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any cause?"
Jesus replied, "Have you not read that the one who created them 'made them male and female' from the beginning?'" (Genesis 1:27)
Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave to his wife; and they shall become one flesh. (Genesis 2:24).
They are no longer two, but one. Let no one separate what God has joined together."
And they asked: "Then why did Moses command that a man may give his wife a bill of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus answered them, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because your hearts are hard. But it was not like this from the beginning.
This is what I am telling you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. And a man should not divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone is capable of accepting the idea of remaining single, but only those who have been given the ability to live without marriage can accept that."
Some men are unable to have children because they were born that way. Others have been made that way by other people. And others have made themselves that way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. And whoever is able to accept living in this way must do so." The little children were brought to Jesus.
And some people brought their little children to Jesus. And they wanted him to lay his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
And Jesus placed his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to obtain eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter the kingdom, keep the commandments."
So the man asked him, "Which ones?" And Jesus said, "You shall not murder, you shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal, you shall not bear false witness.
Honor your father and your mother. Love your neighbor as yourself.
The young man said, "I have obeyed all these commandments. What must I do yet?"
Jesus replied, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. In this way, you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sorrowing. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "Truly I say to you: It is difficult for those who are wealthy to enter the kingdom of the heavens.
And I tell you again: It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
And when the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly amazed. And they said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "This is impossible for mortals, but all things are possible for God."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything and followed you! What then will we have?"
Jesus said to them, "What I tell you is truth. When all things are made new, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. And you will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left houses or families or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more and will inherit eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of the heavens is like a man who was a landowner. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
And he agreed to give them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
He went out again around 9 a.m. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
And he said to them, "Go also, work in my vineyard, and I will give you what is just."
So they departed. "Then he went out again at noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
He went out at about five o'clock and found others still standing around him. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long and do nothing?"
They answered, "Because no one has hired us." He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
And when evening came, the owner of the vineyard said to the head worker, "Call the workers and pay them their wages, beginning with those who came last until you finish with those who came first."
The workers who were hired came in at around five o'clock. Each of them received the usual daily wage.
When those who had worked at the beginning of the day came, they thought that they would receive a greater reward. But each one of them received the wage of a normal day.
And when they received it, they began to complain about its owner.
They said: "These newly hired people worked for just one hour. You paid them the same amount you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
The owner replied to one of them saying: "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual daily wage?
Take your money and go. I want to give those I recently hired the same salary I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel cheated because I gave generously to others?
Therefore, those who are last will become first, and those who are first will become last." Jesus speaks again of his coming death.
Jesus was on his way to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
And he said, "We are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condem him to death.
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. The people will mock him and beat him. They will crucify him on a wooden pole. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" Or do you ask a favor of Jesus
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She fell down on her knees before him and asked him a favor.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked her. And she said, "Promise me that one of my two sons will sit at your right in your kingdom, and the other will sit at your left."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" They said to him, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup, but I cannot say who will sit at my right or left hand, for those places are for those my Father has prepared them for."
And the ten other disciples heard of this, and they were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called them and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them.
Do not be like this, but whoever wants to be great among you, let him be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your servant.
Become like the Son of Man; for he did not come to be served, but to serve and to give his life as a ransom for many." Two blind men recovered their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed him.
There were two blind men sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, "Lord, have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
And their leader, the crowd-stopper, told them to keep quiet and be peaceful. But the two men kept shouting even louder, "Oh God! You, Son of David! Show pity on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them, saying, "What do you want Me to do for you?"
They answered, "Lord, we wish that we could see."
And Jesus was deeply moved with compassion for them. And he touched their eyes. And immediately they regained their sight. And they followed him.
Jesus' Entry into Jerusalem
And when they drew near, all together, to Jerusalem, and came to the Bethsphage, on the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples.
And he said to them, "Go to the village before you, and as soon as you enter it, you will find a tethered female donkey with her foal. Untie them and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This happened so that the prophecy spoken by the prophet would be fulfilled. For it was said:
Say to the daughter of Zion, Behold, your king comes to you, gentle, and riding on an ass, even on a colt the foal of an ass.
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them to do.
They brought a donkey and a young donkey, and they placed their clothes on them. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
Large crowds spread out and placed their coats on the road. Others cut tree branches and spread them on the road.
Some of the people went ahead of him, and others followed him. And they all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
And the crowd answered, saying, "This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple and began to drive out all those who were selling and buying there. And he overturned the tables of the money-changers, and the seats of those who sold doves.
And he said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said: My house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples.' (Isaiah 56: 7) But you have made it a den of robbers." (Jeremiah 7: 11)
The blind and the lame came to Jesus at the temple, and he healed them there.
And the chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the miracles he did, and they heard the children in the temple shouting, " 'O come, let us sing for the Lord; come, let us shout for joy to the Rock of our salvation!' " When they saw this, they were furious.
They asked him, "Do you hear what these children are saying?" Jesus answered them, "Yes, have you never read in the Scriptures: 'From the mouths of children and infants, you have established praise for yourself'?" (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the crowd and went out of the city to the village of Bethany, and there he spent the night. The fig tree's branch dried up.
Jesus was on his way to Jerusalem early in the morning. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road, and went to it, but found nothing on it but leaves. And he said to it, \"May no fruit ever come from you again!\" And immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw it, they marveled, saying, "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you the truth, if you have faith and do not doubt, you will do what was done to the fig tree. Indeed, you will even say to this mountain, 'Go, throw yourself into the sea,' and it will be done.
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for when you pray." Questioning Jesus' authority.
Jesus entered the temple area. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. And they said to him, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
And Jesus answered them, "And I also ask you one question, which if you tell Me, I will also tell you by what authority I do these things.
From where did the baptism of John come? Was it from heaven, or from men?" They disputed with one another, reasoning, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say to us, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say "of the men"? We fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet."
They answered Jesus, saying, "We don't know." And Jesus said to them, "Neither will I tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons
What do you think about this? There was a man who had two sons. So he went to the first one and said to him, My son, go and work today in the vineyard.
"I will not do that," the son answered. But he later changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son and told him the same thing. The son replied, "I will do it, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" And they said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly, I say to you, that tax collectors and prostitutes enter into the kingdom of God before you.
John came to show you the right way to live. And you did not believe in him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes did believe in him. You have seen this. But even after that, you did not turn away from your sins and believe in him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. There was a man who owned a piece of land, so he planted a vineyard in it, surrounded it with a fence, dug a winepress in it, and built a watchtower. Then he rented the vineyard to some farmers, and he went away on a journey.
And when the time of harvest drew near, he sent his servants to the tenants. And he asked the servants to gather his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his servants. And they beat one of them. And they killed another. And they threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. And the tenants treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. And he said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming, and they said to each other, 'This is the one who will inherit all the master of the land's property someday. Come, let's kill him, and everything will be ours.'
And they seized him and took him out of the vineyard and killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do with these tenants?
They answered, " 'These wicked men,' he said, 'will destroy those tenants and give the vineyard to other tenants, who will give him his share of the crop at the harvest.' "
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This was from the Lord, and it is amazing in our eyes?'" (Psalm 118: 22, 23)
So then, this is what I say to you: The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and given to a people that will produce its fruits.
Everyone who falls on that rock will be shattered into pieces. But the rock will crush anyone it falls on.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew he was speaking about them.
So they sought a way to seize him, but they were afraid of the crowds. For the people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
And Jesus told them other stories. And he said:
«Thus is the Kingdom of the heavens. A king took a man, a citizen, and made a marriage feast for his son.
So he sent his servants to those who had been invited to the dinner, and the servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent other servants, saying, Tell those who are invited, Behold, I have prepared my dinner, my oxen and fatlings are killed, and all things are ready. Come to the marriage feast.' '
But the people paid no attention. One went to his field, another to his work.
The rest seized his servants, mistreated them, and then killed them.
The king became very angry. So he sent his army to destroy them. And they killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is prepared, but the guests were not worthy to come."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, whether they were good or evil. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. There he noticed a man who was not dressed in the wedding clothes.
"My friend," he asked, "how did you get in here without the wedding clothes?" The man could not think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet, and throw him outside into the darkness, for there is the weeping and the gnashing of teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and immediately plotted how they might entangle Jesus in what He said.
So they sent to Him His disciples along with the Herodians, and they said to Him, "Teacher, we know that You are truthful, and that You teach the way of God in truth; and You do not care about anyone, for you do not regard the status of people."
Tell us then, what do you think? Is it permissible to pay the tribute to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil plans, and said, "Hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coins that people use to pay taxes." They brought him silver coins.
And he asked them, "Whose is this portrait? And whose are these words?"
They answered, "To Caesar." And he said to them, "Then render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
And when they heard this, they marveled. And they left him and went away. Marriage when the dead rise.
And on that same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. For they do not believe that people will rise from the dead.
"Teacher," they said to him, "this is what Moses said to us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first got married and then died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened with the second, third, and subsequent brothers, continuing until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Now, when the dead rise, whose wife will she be? For they were all married to her, and their number was seven.
Jesus answered them, "You are in error, for you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry, nor will their parents give them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead coming to life? Have you not read what God has told you?
And he said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob" (Exodus 3:6). "He is not the God of the dead, but of the living."
And when the crowds heard this, they were amazed at his teachings. The most important commandment.
When the Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus, the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them, an expert in the law, tested Jesus with a question.
And he said to him, "Teacher, which commandment in the law is the most important?"
Jesus answered, "Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind" (Deuteronomy 6: 5).
This is the first and most important commandment.
And a second similar one: "You shall love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18).
All that is written in the law and the prophets hangs on these two commandments. Who is the son of the Messiah?
The Pharisees met together. And Jesus asked them,
"What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he?" They answered, "The son of David."
And he said to them, "How then does David call him Lord, saying, 'The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at My right hand, till I make Your enemies Your footstool'? For David himself speaks by the Holy Spirit, saying, 'The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at My right hand, till I make Your enemies Your footstool.'"
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool."
If David calls him Lord, how can he be David's son?"
No one was able to reply to him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any other question.
Jesus judged the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
And He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they ask of you. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They bind heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of others. But they themselves do not wish to move a single finger to transfer them.
All they do is for others to see. They put little boxes on their foreheads and arms, containing verses from the Bible. And they make these boxes very wide. And they make the tassels on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at feasts, and they love to be greeted in the markets with the best greetings, and that they have the best seats in the temples.
They love to be greeted in the markets, and they love to be called rabbis.
But do not call anyone on earth 'rabbi,' for you have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth your father, for the only Father you have is in heaven.
You should not be called "Teacher." You have one Teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled. And whoever is humbled will be exalted.
"Alas for you teachers, you hypocrites! You teachers of the law and you Pharisees! For you lock up the kingdom of heaven. For you do not enter, nor will you let those enter who are entering. "
Translate
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you cross sea and land to make a single proselyte, and when he becomes one, you make him twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
"O blind guides, who are spiritually bankrupt! You say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing. But if anyone swears by the gold in the temple, they must do what they promised.'"
You are blind and foolish! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or the temple which makes the gold sacred?
And you also say, "If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing; but if anyone swears by the gift on the altar, he must fulfill his vow."
Blind guides! Which is more important? The gift, or the altar that makes the gift sacred?
"Whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it."
And everyone who swears by the name of the Temple, swears by it and by the one who dwells in it.
And everyone who swears by the name of heaven has sworn by the throne of God and by the one who sits upon it.
"O you, what teachers you are of the Law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You give to God a tenth of your mint, dill and cumin, but you have neglected the more important matters of the Law: justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced righteousness without neglecting any of those other things."
Blind guides! You strain out a gnat and swallow a camel whole!
Oh, what teachers of the law and Pharisees you are! Hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and dish, but inside you are full of greed and wickedness. Blind Pharisees! What terrible things you will be judged for!
You blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and the plate, so that the outside of them may also become clean.
Oh, the horror of what you are doing, you teachers instructing in the law! And you, Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which appear beautiful on the outside, but inside are full of the bones of the dead and also full of other unclean and impure things.
The same applies to you. From the outside, you appear to be doing what is right. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend that you are not what you are.
Woe to you teachers of the law and you Pharisees, hypocrites! Woe to you, evil men! You build the tombs of the prophets and you decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you would say, 'If only we had lived in the time of those who came before us, we would not have done what they did, and we would not have helped in the killing of the prophets.'
You bear witness against yourselves. You acknowledge that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
So complete what those before you from the believers started, of disobedience that which was initiated by those before you from your forefathers.
O nest of deadly vipers! How will you escape being condemned to hell?
Therefore I will send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, crucify some of them, and flog others in your synagogues and pursue them from town to town.
You will pay the price for all the blood of the righteous that has been shed on earth. I mean, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berekiah. And Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I will tell you is a reality. All of this will happen to those who are living now.
"Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you, I have often desired to gather your children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, but you were not willing!"
Look, your home has been left empty.
I tell you: You will not see me henceforth until you say: 'Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord' (Psalm 118:26).
End of the world signs
Jesus left the temple. As he was walking away, his disciples approached him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
And Jesus asked: "Do you see all these things? Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left here upon another, but will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. And there came to him the disciples privately. And they said to him: "Tell us, when will this be? And what will be the sign of your coming? And what will be the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered them, "Be careful! Guard against the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Many will come in my name and will say, 'I am the Messiah!' And they will deceive many people.
You will hear of wars. You will also hear people talking about wars to come. Do not be afraid. These things must happen. But the end is not yet. (Note: The original Arabic text is: "ستسمعون عن حروب. كما ستسمعون الناس يتحدثون عن حروب مستقبلية. لا تخافوا. يجب أن تحدث هذه الأمور. لكن النهاية لم تأت بعد.")
Nation will fight against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be great earthquakes, famines and pestilences in various places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
"Then the people will deliver you up to be afflicted, and to be killed, and you will be hated by all nations for my name's sake."
And at that time, many will fall away from their faith. They will hate one another. They will deliver each other to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear and deceive many people.
Because lawlessness will increase, the love of most will grow cold.
But the one who stands firm to the end, that one will be saved.
This good news about the kingdom will be proclaimed throughout the world, serving as a testimony for all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that causes desolation." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) For at a future time, he will be standing in the holy place. And the reader must understand this.
Then those in Judaism should escape to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get anything from it.
No one in the field should go back to get their coat.
Woe on that day to pregnant women, and woe on that day to nursing mothers.
Pray so that you don't have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than at any time since the beginning of the world until now. And there will not be anything like it again ever.
If time were not shortened, no one would survive. But because of the chosen people of God, it will be shortened.
And at that time, someone might say to you, 'Look, here is the Messiah!' or 'There he is!' But do not believe it.
False messiahs, antichrists, and lying prophets will appear and perform great signs and wonders, and they will try to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Did you see? I told you beforehand.
If someone says to you, 'Look, here is the Messiah!' in the desert, do not go there. And if someone says, 'Look, here he is in the inner room!' do not believe it.
The lightning that appears in the east can be seen in the west; so it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever a carcass is found.
"Immediately following the terrible ordeal of those days, 'The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood.' (Joel 2:31; Acts 2:20) And it is written, 'Immediately after the tribulation of those days, 'the heaven will pass away with a great roar, and the elements will melt with fervent heat; both the earth and the works that are done on it will be burned up.' (2 Peter 3:10-12) For the Lord God omnipotent reigns." (Revelation 11:15)
And at that time the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the tribes of the earth will mourn. And they will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven, with power and great glory.
His angels shall be sent with a great sound of a trumpet, and they shall gather his elect from the four winds, from one end of heaven to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. As soon as its branches become soft and it puts out leaves, you know that summer is near.
Likewise, when you see all these things happening, know that the end is near. It is at hand.
What I am going to tell you is a fact. The people who are living at that time will certainly not die until all these things happen.
The heavens and the earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away. Concerning that day and hour no one knows.
No one knows that day or that hour, not even the angels in heaven know it. The Son does not know, only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all these things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They had no idea what was going to happen until the flood came and swept them all away. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
Two women will grind together; one will be taken and the other will be left.
Wait therefore, ye know not in what day your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Let's suppose the homeowner knew exactly what time of night the thief would come. Then he would watch. He would not let his house be robbed.
Therefore you also must be ready, for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Let us suppose that a master has appointed one of his servants to be in charge of the other servants in his house. And the servant's task is to provide them with their food at the proper time. And the master wants a faithful and wise servant to do this.
It would be good for the slave if the master found him doing his work upon the master's return.
What I am going to tell you is a fact. The master will put that servant in charge of all his possessions.
"But suppose that slave is wicked. And suppose he said to himself, 'My master is going to be away for a very long time.'"
Suppose he began to strike the other servants. And suppose that he eats and drinks with those who drink heavily.
The master of that servant will come on a day when the servant does not expect him. He will return at an hour that the servant does not know.
Then the master will cut him into pieces, and send him to the place where the hypocrites go. And there they will weep and grind their teeth.
The Story of the Ten Maids of Honour
Thus shall be the Kingdom of Heaven. Ten virgins took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were fools. Five of them were wise.
As for the ignorant women, they took their lamps and did not take oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. Therefore, the bridesmaids felt tired and went to sleep.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted: 'Here is the groom! Come out to meet him!'
Then the bride's maidens all awoke and prepared their lamps.
The foolish said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are about to go out."
They answered, "No, there may not be enough for all of us. Rather, you should go to those who sell the oil and buy some for yourselves."
They went to buy the oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. And the bridesmaids who were ready with him went into the wedding dinner. Then the door was shut.
Then the other bridesmaids came later. And they said, "Sir! Sir! Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Be on guard, because you do not know the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. The story of three slaves.
And again, this is what the kingdom of heaven will be like. There was a man traveling, who called his servants and entrusted his possessions to them.
Someone gave 10 thousand dollars. And the other gave 4 thousand dollars. And the third gave 2 thousand dollars. The man gave each servant the amount he knew the servant could take care of. Then he set off on his journey.
The servant who received the ten thousand dollars immediately went and invested his money. He earned another ten thousand dollars.
The person who was earning 4000 dollars received an additional 4000 dollars.
But the man who received two thousand dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. And he buried his master's money there.
And after a long time, the master of those slaves returned. And he wanted to gather all the money that they had earned.
And the man who had received $10,000 brought another $10,000 with him. He said, "Sir, you entrusted me with $10,000. Look, I have earned another $10,000."
His master replied, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things, so I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share in your master's happiness!"
And the man who had $4000 also came. And he said, "Master, you have trusted me with $4000. Look, I have gained an additional $4000."
His master replied, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things, so I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share in your master's happiness!"
Then the man to whom two thousand dollars had been given came. And he said, "Sir, I knew that you were a hard man. You reap where you have not sown, and gather where you have not scattered.
So you were afraid. You went out and buried your $2000 in the ground. See, that's what comes back to you.'
Then the master answered him, 'You wicked and lazy servant! Did you know that I reap where I did not sow, and gather where I did not scatter?
Well, then you should have put my money in the bank. And when I came back, I would have gotten it back again with interest.
Then his master said to the other servants, "Take the two thousand dollars from him. And give them to the one who has twenty thousand dollars."
Everyone who has will be given more; they will have more than enough. What about the person who has nothing? Even what little they have will be taken away.
Cast out that worthless servant into the outer darkness. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.' The sheep and the goats.
The Son of Man will come in his glory, and all the angels will come with him. Then he will sit on his throne, and all the nations will be gathered before him. And he will separate them one from another, as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats. And he will put the sheep on his right, but the goats on the left. Then the King will say to those on his right, 'Come, you who are blessed by my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.' And to those on the left he will say, 'Depart from me, all you who are under God's anger, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.' And these will go away into eternal punishment, but the righteous into eternal life.
All nations will be gathered before him, and he will separate people into two groups, just like a shepherd who separates the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the King will say to those on His right, 'Come, you who are blessed by my Father, inherit the Kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.'
I was hungry. And you gave me food. I was thirsty. And you gave me drink. I was a stranger. And you took me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then the righteous who did good deeds will answer him, saying, "Our Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you? And when did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?"
When did we see you as a stranger and take you in? When did we see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
When have we ever seen you sick or in prison and we went to visit you?
And the king will answer, 'What I have told you is the truth. Whatever you did for one of these least important brothers of mine, you did it for me.'"
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Go away from Me, you accursed ones, into the everlasting fire prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger, and you did not take me in. I needed clothes, and you did not give me anything. I was sick and I was in prison, and you did not take care of me.
And they will also answer: "Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? When did we see you as a stranger or in need of clothing or sick or in prison and did not help you?"
"Then he will answer them, 'I tell you the truth: whatever you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for me either.'"
"Then they depart from there to eternal punishment, while the righteous obtain eternal life."
The plan to kill Jesus.
And after Jesus finished all these sayings, He said to His disciples:
As you know, the Passover is two days away. And the Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They planned to seize Jesus by cunning, for they wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "Or the people may become stirred up." A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with an alabaster flask of very costly fragrant oil, pure perfume. She poured it on his head as he sat at the table.
And when the disciples saw it, they were indignant, saying, "Why this waste?"
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus knew this. He said to them, "Why do you trouble the woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me.
You will always have the poor with you, but you will not always have me.
I poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What I have done will be told everywhere the good news is preached throughout the world. It will be told in memory of her." Judas agrees to hand Jesus over.
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
And he asked them, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" And they paid him thirty pieces of silver.
And since then, Judas had been watching for an opportune time to hand Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
This was the first day of Unleavened Bread. And the disciples came to Jesus, saying to Him, "Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?"
He answered, "Go into the city to So-and-so and say to him, 'The Teacher says, My appointed time has drawn near, and I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them to do, and they prepared the Passover meal.
And when evening came, Jesus was sitting at the table with the twelve.
And as they were eating, he said, "Truly I tell you, one of you will betray me."
And the disciples were very sorrowful, and they began to say to him one after another, "Is it I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand with me in the bowl is the one who will betray me."
"For the Son of Man is going just as it is written about him, but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for that man if he had not been born."
And Judas was he that would betray him. And he said, "Master, is it I?" And Jesus said unto him, "Thou hast said. That thou art."
And while they were eating, Jesus took bread, and after blessing it he broke it and gave it to his disciples and said, "Take, eat; this is my body."
Then he took the cup, praised Allah (exalted is he) and gave it to them. He said, "Drink, all of you, from it."
This is my blood, the blood of the new covenant, shed for many for the forgiveness of sins.
This is what I say to you: I will not drink of the fruit of the vine with you until the day when I drink it with you new in the kingdom of my Father.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus said that Peter would fail.
Jesus said to them, "All of you will be made to stumble because of me this night. For it is written, 'And the Lord said, "I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered."' (Zechariah 13:7)"
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.'
Peter answered, "All may fall away because of you, but not I, for I will never fall away."
Jesus answered, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. This very night before the rooster crows, you yourself will deny knowing me three times."
But Peter said, "I must die with you. I will never say that I don't know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus' Prayer in Gethsemane
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemane. And he said to them, "Sit here, while I go there and pray."
And he took with him Peter and the sons of Zebedee. And he began to feel sorrow and distress.
Then He said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful, even to death. Stay here and watch with Me."
He went a little further. Then he fell on his face to the ground. And he prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but thine, be done."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Could you not watch with me for one hour?"
"Watch and pray, so that you will not give in to temptation. For the spirit is willing, but the body is weak."
Jesus went away again. And he prayed, saying, "Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not as I will, but as thou wilt."
Then he came back. And again he found them asleep. They could not keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went again. And he prayed for the third time the same prayer.
Then he returned to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look, the hour is near, and the Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of sinners."
Arise! Let us go! Here he is, the one who is delivering me over to them, he has come!" The arrest of Jesus
And as Jesus was speaking, Judas arrived, being one of the twelve. With him was a large crowd carrying swords and clubs, having been sent by the chief priests and the elders of the people.
Judas, who was about to betray Jesus, had agreed upon a signal with them. He said, "The man I will kiss is the one; seize him."
Immediately Judas went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Rabbi!" And he kissed him.
Jesus replied, "My friend, do as you have come to do." Then the soldiers approached, seized Jesus, and arrested him.
And at that moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword. He struck out and cut off the ear of the high priest's servant.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place, for all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how do the prophecies mentioned in the holy books come true then? They say that this must happen in this way.
And at that time Jesus spoke to the crowd. And he asked them, "Was I leading a band of armed men against you? Were you to come with swords and clubs to arrest me? I was sitting every day in the temple courts teaching. And you did not arrest me."
"But all this happened so that the prophecies of the prophets would be fulfilled." At that moment, all the disciples left him and fled. Jesus is taken to the Sanhedrin Council
And those who had arrested Jesus led him to Caiaphas, the high priest. The teachers of the law and the elders had assembled there.
Peter was not far away, for he was following Jesus, and he went straight to the courtyard of the high priest. He entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
And the chief priests and the whole council were seeking something to condemn Jesus by. For they desired to put him to death.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses came forward to testify. Finally, two other witnesses came forward to testify.
They said: "This man said: 'I am able to destroy the temple of God and rebuild it in three days.'"
Then the high priest stood up, and asked Jesus, "Are you not going to answer? What is it that these men testify against you?"
But Jesus held His peace. Then the high priest said unto him, I adjure thee by the living God, that thou tell us, whether thou be the Christ, the Son of God? (Note: This translation includes verses 63 and 64 from Matthew 26, which directly follows the provided text in most manuscripts and early versions of the Bible.)
Jesus answered, "Yes, just as you said, it is so. But I tell you all: From now on you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Powerful, and you will see him coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his robes and said, "He has spoken blasphemous words against God! Why do we still need witnesses? You have heard him speak these blasphemous words."
What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they spat in his face. And they hit him with their fists. And others slapped him.
They said, "Prophesy to us, Christ, who is it that struck you?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him and said to him, "You also were with Jesus the Galilean."
But Peter, in front of everyone, said he wasn't. "I don't know what you're talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate leading to the courtyard. And there another woman saw him. And she said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
And he said again that it was not so. He swore, "I do not know this man!"
And after a little while, those who stood there went to Peter and said to him, "You are surely one of them, for the way you speak betrays you."
Then Peter began to curse himself and he began to swear to them, saying, "I do not know this man!" And immediately a rooster crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him: "Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times." And Peter went out and wept bitterly.
Judas hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. And all the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled to kill Jesus.
So they seized him and led him away, and then they handed him over to Pilate, who was the governor.
And Judas, the one who betrayed him, saw that Jesus had been condemned to death. He felt deep remorse and sorrow for what he had done. He brought back the three silver coins to the chief priests and elders.
"I have sinned," he said. "I have handed over an innocent man." "What do we care?" they answered. "That's your problem."
Then Judas threw the silver into the temple and departed, and went and hanged himself.
So the chief priests took the silver and said, "It is not right to put it into the treasury, for it is the price of blood."
They decided to use the money to buy a pottery field. People from other countries were to be buried there.
And for this reason, it has been called the Field of Blood down to the present day.
Then the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled, for he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which the sons of Israel had set a value for him.
Then they took the silver and bought the potter's field, as the Lord commanded me." (Zechariah 11:12, 13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9). The Presentation of Jesus at the Temple.
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the chief priests and the elders accused him, he gave no answer.
Then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the testimony they are giving against you?"
But Jesus answered nothing, not even to a single charge. The governor was exceedingly astonished at this.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose the one they wanted.
And at that time they had a famous prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate asked them, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were jealous. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
And while Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message saying, "Have nothing to do with that righteous man, for I have suffered much because of him today."
But the chief priests and elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas to be released and Jesus to be executed.
"Which of the two do you want me to release for you?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
So Pilate asked, "What then shall I do with Jesus who is called the Christ?" All the people answered, "Let him be crucified!"
"Why? What evil has he done?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he had not achieved anything. On the contrary, the crowd began to get angry. He took water and washed his hands in front of them. And he said, "I am not responsible for the death of this man." "You are responsible for it!"
All the people answered: "We and our children will bear the guilt for his death!"
Pilate released Barabbas, but ordered Jesus to be flogged, and then handed him over to be crucified on the cross. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorian. All the other soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him and dressed him in a purple robe.
Then they twisted the thorns together to make a crown. And they put it on his head. And they put a staff in his right hand. Then they knelt down before him and mocked him. And they said, "We salute you, O King of the Jews!"
They spat on him. And they hit him on the head with a stick again and again and again.
And after they mocked him, they took the robe off him. They put his own clothes on him again. Then they led him out to crucify him with nails. Jesus was crucified on the cross.
And as they were going out of the city, they encountered a man from القيروان whose name was Simon. And they forced him to carry the cross.
And they came to a place called Golgotha. And the word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
And in it they mixed the wine with bitter herbs, and they offered it to Jesus to drink. But after he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
And when they crucified him, they divided his garments among them, casting lots for them.
They sat there and kept watching him.
And they placed over his head the accusation written against him, which read: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
Two robbers were crucified with him, one on his right and the other on his left.
And the passersby were shouting in the face of Jesus and mocking him. And they shook their heads.
And they said, «Are you going to destroy the sanctuary and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! If you are the Son of God, come down from the cross!».
Similarly, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders ridiculed him.
"He saved others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross, and we will believe in him."
He believes in God, so let God save him now if he wants him, for he is the one who said, "I am the Son of God."
In the same way, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus also mocked him. The death of Jesus.
And from noon to the third hour, darkness came over the whole land.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" which means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
And some of those standing there heard Jesus cry out and said, "He is calling Elijah."
Immediately someone ran and brought a sponge, filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed, and offered it to Jesus to drink.
And the rest said, "Leave him alone and let us see if Elijah will come to save him."
And after Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
And at that moment, the curtain of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. And the earth shook. And the rocks split.
The tombs were opened. And the bodies of many saints who had died were raised to life.
They came out of the tombs. And after Jesus rose from the dead, they went to the holy city. And there they appeared to many people.
And the Roman commander and those guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened, and they were filled with awe and said, "Truly, this was the Son of God!"
There was a large number of women watching what was happening from a short distance away. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
And Mary Magdalene was with them. And there were also Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried.
And when it was almost evening, there came a rich man from Arimathea, named Joseph, who himself had become a disciple of Jesus.
And he went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus. Pilate commanded that it be given to him.
And Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen shroud.
And he placed it in his new tomb which he had hewn in the rock. And he rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he went away.
And Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there, opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb.
The next day was the day after the preparation day. So the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
They said, "Lord, we remember that deceiver said while he was alive, 'After three days I will rise.' "
Give orders to secure the tomb until the third day, otherwise his disciples may come and steal the body, then tell the people that Jesus has risen from the dead, and this last lie will be worse than the first.
Pilate answered, "Take a guard with you and go and make the tomb as secure as you can."
They went and secured the grave, sealed the stone, and placed guards for surveillance.
Jesus rose from the dead.
Saturday is now past. The first day of the week had come, at the dawn. And Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a great earthquake. And the angel of the Lord descended from heaven. And the angel went to the tomb. And he rolled away the stone and sat upon it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, just as he said! Come and see the place where he was lying.
Go quickly! And tell his disciples, 'He has been raised from the dead, and he is going before you to Galilee; there you will see him.' Behold, I have told you.'
The two women hurried away from the tomb, and they were afraid and very happy. They ran to tell the disciples.
And behold, Jesus met them and said, "Peace be to you!" They approached and took hold of His feet and worshiped Him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The Guard's Report to the Chief Priests
And as the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city and reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they came up with a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
And they said to the soldiers, "We want you to say, 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the governor hears about this report, we will pay him off. This will save you from trouble."
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has been spread among Jews to this day. The last command that Jesus gave to his disciples.
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. And they went up to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
And when they saw him, they worshipped him. But some still doubted.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Therefore, go and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
And teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And know that I am always with you, to the end."
John the Baptist paves the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
The prophet Isaiah wrote long ago: "I will send my messenger ahead of you, and he will prepare your way." (Malachi 3: 1)
A voice cries out in the wilderness: Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight paths for our God in the desert.
And so John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. He also preached to people about the necessity of being baptized and repenting of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
And all the inhabitants of the countryside of Judea went out to him, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem. And they confessed their sins, and he baptized them in the river Jordan.
John was wearing clothes made of camel hair. He was wearing a leather belt around his waist. He was eating locusts and wild honey.
This is what John was proclaiming: "One is coming after me who is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to untie."
I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism of Jesus and his temptation.
And at that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee, and was baptized by John in the River Jordan.
And Jesus was baptized, and immediately he saw the heavens opened, and he saw the Spirit of God descending upon him like a dove.
And a voice from heaven called out to him, saying, "You are my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
And immediately, the Holy Spirit sent Jesus into the wilderness.
Jesus was in the desert for 40 days. There the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
And after John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Galilee, preaching the gospel of God.
"It is time," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
Jesus was once walking by the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets at once and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further ahead. And as he walked, he saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat mending their nets.
Immediately he called them. So they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and followed Jesus. Jesus Casts Out a Demon
And Jesus, and those that were with him, went into Capernaum. And when the sabbath day was come, he went into the synagogue, and began to teach there.
And the people were astonished at his teaching, for he was teaching them as one having authority, and he was not like the teachers of the law.
And at that time there was a man in the synagogue who had an unclean spirit, and he cried out, saying,
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are: the Holy One of God!"
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
And all the people were amazed. And they said to one another, "What is this? For a new teaching with authority! Even the spirits of demons are subject to him!"
The news about Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
And Jesus, and those that were with him, departed from the synagogue. And immediately they went away with James and John into the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed, suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, and helped her up. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
And in the evening, after the sun had set, all the sick people were brought to Jesus, and all those who were possessed by demons were also brought to him.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also drove out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus' prayer in a quiet place
It was very early in the morning, and the darkness was still there. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. And there he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
And when they found him, they cried out, saying, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered them, "Let us go to another place, to the nearby villages, because I must preach there also. For this I have come."
So he traveled throughout Galilee, preaching in their synagogues and also casting out demons. Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down before Jesus, begging him. He said, "If you are willing to make me 'clean,' you are able to do it."
Jesus was filled with profound anxiety. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
The illness left him immediately. He was cured.
Jesus immediately drove him out. He gave the man a strong warning.
And he said, "Do not tell anyone about this, but go and present yourself to the priest, and offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to the priest and the people that you are 'pure.'"
But the man went out and began to talk at once. And he spread the news to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter the city publicly. And he remained outside in secluded places. But the people kept coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
And after a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And the people heard that he had returned home.
A large crowd of people had gathered to the point that there was no more room. There wasn't even room outside the door. And Jesus was preaching the word to them.
And four of those present were carrying a man who was unable to walk.
But they couldn't get him to Jesus because of the crowd. So they opened a space in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his mat through the opening.
And Jesus, seeing their faith, said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven you."
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why does this man speak in this way? He is saying something very wicked! Only God can forgive sins!
And Jesus immediately knew what they were thinking. And He said to them, "Why do you reason about this?
Is it easier for me to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then Jesus said to the man...
"I'm telling you," he said, "get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man got up and took his mat. Then he walked away, while everyone was watching him. And all the people were amazed. And they praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
And Jesus went again by the Sea of Galilee. And a great multitude gathered unto him, and he began to teach them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax collection site. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi rose and followed him.
Jesus later had dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
And there were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that and said to them, "The healthy do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to call those who think they are righteous before God to follow me, but I came to call sinners to follow me." Jesus was asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting, but not eating. And some people came to Jesus. And they said to him, "The disciples of John fast. And the disciples of the Pharisees fast. But your disciples do not fast. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as the bridegroom is with them.
But a time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; and in that day they shall not eat anything.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth on old clothes. And if they did, the new piece would tear away from the old piece. This would make the tear even worse.
People don't put new wine into old wine bottles. Otherwise, the wine would burst the bottles. And the wine and the bottles would both be ruined. No, everyone puts new wine into new wine bottles." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the wheat fields. The disciples began to pick some wheat heads.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! This man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath. Why then do your disciples do this?"
He answered, "Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. And David gave some of it to his men too.
Then Jesus said to them: "The Sabbath was made for man's sake, and not man for the Sabbath's sake."
For the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day."
Healing on Saturday
And Jesus went again to the synagogue, and there was a man whose hand was withered and paralyzed.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with the weak and twisted hand. And he said to him, "Stand in front of everyone."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say about what we should do on the Sabbath? Should we do good or evil? Should we save life or kill?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them with anger, those around him. He was very disturbed because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, as healthy as the other one.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus went with His disciples to the Sea of Galilee, and a great multitude followed Him from Galilee.
The people heard about all the things Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and Idumea, and from the region east of the Jordan River, and from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
And because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This was to prevent people from crowding around him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick would rush forward to touch him.
And when people who had evil spirits saw him, they fell down before him. And the spirits cried out, saying, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them to tell no one about him. Jesus' appointment of the twelve apostles
Jesus went up into the mountain, and called unto him certain people whom he named, and they came unto him.
And he appointed twelve from among them, whom he called apostles. And from that time they were with him. And he sent them out to preach.
And they will have the power to cast out demons.
Jesus chose the twelve, and Simon was one of them. Jesus named him Peter.
And there were James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus surnamed them Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus. And there was Thaddaeus, and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was also one of them. He was the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered one of the homes. Once again, a crowd of people gathered. The crowd was so large that even Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this, so they came to take him, saying that he was crazy.
And there were some teachers of the law who came down from Jerusalem, and they said, "By Beelzebul, the prince of demons, he is casting out demons!"
Jesus called them to Him and spoke to them about the way of life, saying, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If the kingdom fights against itself, it cannot stand.
If a family splits, it cannot endure.
